===Glyph origin===
{{Han etyl}}
{{liushu|i}} - an arrowhead indicating "to enter".

Compare {{zh-l|人}}.

===Etymology===
From {{inh|zh|sit-pro|*nup ~ nip}}. Cognate with {{cog|bo|ནུབ||to fall (gradually), sink, to set; to decay, to decline}} and {{cog|my|နိပ်||to decline (e.g. price); to be suppressed, to settle, to be quelled}}.

Chinese characters belonging to the same word family include:
* {{och-l|納|to make enter}}
* {{och-l|內|inside}}
* {{och-l|軜|reins on the inside of the horse}}
* {{och-l|妠|to marry (a girl)}}
* {{och-l|躡|to tiptoe, tread}}
* {{och-l|攝|to take in, photograph, grasp}}
* {{och-l|鑷|tweezers, forceps}}
* and potentially {{cog|yue|-}} {{zh-m|*擸|tr=laap3|to bring together, to collect}}, as well as {{och-l|退|to withdraw, retire}}

The regular Mandarin pronunciation as predicted from Middle Chinese is ''rì''. The irregular sound change is for taboo reasons - to avoid homophony with its derived vulgar meaning "to enter > to have sexual intercourse", nowadays represented by {{zh-l|日}}.

